---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro'

export const prerender = true

const slug = 'features'
const title = 'Features: Built for Production'
const eyebrow = 'Features'
const heading = "Everything you need. Nothing you don't."
const description =
  'Silkweave features: Zod-powered schemas, built-in OAuth 2.1, auto-generated Swagger, typed context, streaming actions, Vercel AI SDK support, serverless deploys, a CLI, and build-time type generation.'

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://www.silkweave.dev' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Features', item: 'https://www.silkweave.dev/features' }
  ]
}
---

<PageLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  slug={slug}
  eyebrow={eyebrow}
  heading={heading}
  intro="Silkweave is small on purpose, but the things you actually need to ship a real server are built in - validation, auth, context, streaming, and docs - and they work the same across every transport because they live in the action layer, not the adapter."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <h2 id="schemas-and-types">Schemas and types, from one declaration</h2>
  <p>
    Inputs and outputs are <strong>Zod schemas</strong>, so a single declaration gives you runtime
    validation, static type inference, and generated documentation. There is no second source of
    truth to keep in sync. <a href="/docs#actions">Actions</a> thread those types all the way to the
    edges: the tRPC adapter infers a typed router, Fastify emits OpenAPI, and the
    <a href="/docs#adapter-typegen">typegen</a> adapter writes <code>.d.ts</code> interfaces from your schemas
    at build time - zero runtime cost.
  </p>

  <h2 id="auth">Authentication, built in</h2>
  <p>
    <code>@silkweave/auth</code> ships a full <strong>OAuth 2.1</strong> proxy - PKCE, refresh tokens,
    dynamic client registration, and protected resource metadata (RFC 9728) - plus simple bearer-token
    validation when that is all you need. It is the same auth layer across MCP HTTP, REST, and tRPC.
    See <a href="/docs#authentication">authentication</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="context">Typed context, no globals</h2>
  <p>
    Every action receives a typed <strong>context</strong> bag - logger, auth, request metadata -
    via a fluent <code>.set()</code> API. No module-level singletons, no hidden globals: what an
    action can see is exactly what you passed it. Read more on <a href="/docs#context">context</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="streaming">Streaming and AI</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Streaming actions</strong> yield chunks from an async generator, and each adapter delivers
    them appropriately - MCP progress, SSE, NDJSON, or tRPC subscriptions
    (<a href="/docs#streaming">streaming docs</a>). On top of that, the
    <a href="/docs#integration-ai-sdk">Vercel AI SDK bridge</a> wires <code>useChat</code> straight to
    a streaming action over a tRPC subscription - typed <code>UIMessageChunk</code>s, no Data Stream
    Protocol, no bespoke chat route.
  </p>

  <h2 id="deploy-anywhere">Deploy anywhere</h2>
  <p>
    Expose the same actions as a <a href="/docs#adapter-cli">CLI</a> with interactive prompts, ship a
    stateless MCP server to <a href="/docs#adapter-vercel">Vercel, Cloudflare Workers, or Bun</a> on
    Web Standard APIs, or serve an auto-generated <a href="/docs#adapter-fastify">Swagger UI</a> from
    your REST adapter. One codebase, every surface your users reach for.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next">Keep exploring</h2>
  <ul class="next-links">
    <li><a href="/how-it-works">How it works &rarr;<br /><span>The Action model</span></a></li>
    <li><a href="/adapters">Adapters &rarr;<br /><span>Every transport</span></a></li>
    <li><a href="/docs">Full documentation &rarr;<br /><span>API reference</span></a></li>
  </ul>
</PageLayout>
